1
00:00:11,250 --> 00:00:12,827
January 15th,

2
00:00:12,827 --> 00:00:13,640
1919

3
00:00:13,640 --> 00:00:16,240
A strangely warm winter day in Boston

4
00:00:16,611 --> 00:00:17,721
In the busy North End,

5
00:00:17,721 --> 00:00:18,481
children play,

6
00:00:18,481 --> 00:00:19,481
workers eat lunch,

7
00:00:19,481 --> 00:00:22,081
and horses pull wagons along the waterfront

8
00:00:22,348 --> 00:00:22,858
Suddenly,

9
00:00:22,858 --> 00:00:24,258
people hear a deep rumble,

10
00:00:24,258 --> 00:00:26,630
and a sound like machine guns firing

11
00:00:26,780 --> 00:00:28,170
A giant tank bursts open,

12
00:00:28,170 --> 00:00:29,410
and a wave of molasses,

13
00:00:29,410 --> 00:00:30,490
taller than a house,

14
00:00:30,490 --> 00:00:32,096
rushes through the streets

15
00:00:32,246 --> 00:00:33,076
It sounds like a joke

16
00:00:33,076 --> 00:00:35,236
But it was one of the strangest and deadliest

17
00:00:35,236 --> 00:00:37,316
disasters in American history

18
00:00:37,573 --> 00:00:39,203
And the company behind it tried to blame

19
00:00:39,203 --> 00:00:40,596
someone else

20
00:00:40,746 --> 00:00:43,016
This is the true story of the Great Molasses

21
00:00:43,016 --> 00:00:44,136
Flood

22
00:00:47,417 --> 00:00:48,767
In 1915,

23
00:00:48,767 --> 00:00:51,673
a company built a giant steel tank on Boston's

24
00:00:51,673 --> 00:00:52,793
waterfront

25
00:00:53,060 --> 00:00:55,130
It belonged to a company called United States

26
00:00:55,130 --> 00:00:56,410
Industrial Alcohol,

27
00:00:56,410 --> 00:00:58,050
and stood on Commercial Street,

28
00:00:58,050 --> 00:00:59,621
in the North End

29
00:00:59,771 --> 00:01:01,841
It was about fifty feet tall and ninety feet

30
00:01:01,841 --> 00:01:02,161
wide,

31
00:01:02,161 --> 00:01:04,281
and it could hold more than two million gallons

32
00:01:04,281 --> 00:01:05,705
of molasses

33
00:01:05,855 --> 00:01:08,085
That is enough to fill more than three Olympic

34
00:01:08,085 --> 00:01:09,525
swimming pools

35
00:01:09,729 --> 00:01:10,889
Molasses is the thick,

36
00:01:10,889 --> 00:01:13,329
dark syrup left over when sugar is made from

37
00:01:13,329 --> 00:01:14,704
sugar cane

38
00:01:14,854 --> 00:01:16,724
Molasses was turned into alcohol,

39
00:01:16,724 --> 00:01:17,764
used for drinks,

40
00:01:17,764 --> 00:01:19,924
and for making explosives during the First

41
00:01:19,924 --> 00:01:21,241
World War

42
00:01:21,391 --> 00:01:22,141
During the war,

43
00:01:22,141 --> 00:01:23,261
business was booming,

44
00:01:23,261 --> 00:01:25,861
and the company wanted more and more of it

45
00:01:26,140 --> 00:01:28,170
Ships from the Caribbean filled the tank again

46
00:01:28,170 --> 00:01:29,462
and again

47
00:01:29,612 --> 00:01:31,722
And right next to it lived thousands of people,

48
00:01:31,722 --> 00:01:33,562
in one of the busiest neighborhoods in the

49
00:01:33,562 --> 00:01:34,663
city

50
00:01:34,843 --> 00:01:37,163
Many of them were Italian immigrant families,

51
00:01:37,163 --> 00:01:39,363
crowded into small apartments just a few

52
00:01:39,363 --> 00:01:40,803
streets away

53
00:01:44,450 --> 00:01:45,880
The tank was built in a hurry,

54
00:01:45,880 --> 00:01:48,200
overseen by a company treasurer with no

55
00:01:48,200 --> 00:01:49,640
engineering training

56
00:01:50,174 --> 00:01:52,604
He never asked an engineer or an architect to

57
00:01:52,604 --> 00:01:54,146
check the plans

58
00:01:54,296 --> 00:01:55,646
It was never properly tested

59
00:01:55,646 --> 00:01:57,766
They filled it with only a few inches of

60
00:01:57,766 --> 00:01:58,814
water

61
00:01:58,964 --> 00:02:00,914
A proper test would have meant filling it with

62
00:02:00,914 --> 00:02:01,274
water,

63
00:02:01,274 --> 00:02:03,017
all the way to the top

64
00:02:03,167 --> 00:02:03,997
Almost right away,

65
00:02:03,997 --> 00:02:05,337
the tank began to leak

66
00:02:05,337 --> 00:02:07,848
Brown streaks ran down its sides

67
00:02:07,998 --> 00:02:10,068
Local children collected the dripping molasses

68
00:02:10,068 --> 00:02:11,889
as a free sweet treat

69
00:02:12,039 --> 00:02:13,949
A worker who looked after the tank warned his

70
00:02:13,949 --> 00:02:15,069
boss about the leaks,

71
00:02:15,069 --> 00:02:17,149
and strange cracking sounds

72
00:02:17,531 --> 00:02:19,715
He was told that nothing was wrong

73
00:02:19,865 --> 00:02:20,735
The company's answer?

74
00:02:20,735 --> 00:02:21,975
They painted the tank brown,

75
00:02:21,975 --> 00:02:24,474
so the leaks would be harder to see

76
00:02:24,624 --> 00:02:25,334
The leaks were hidden

77
00:02:25,334 --> 00:02:27,414
The danger was not

78
00:02:30,850 --> 00:02:32,880
In January 1919,

79
00:02:32,880 --> 00:02:35,480
a ship arrived with a fresh load of molasses

80
00:02:36,156 --> 00:02:38,226
Warm molasses from the ship was pumped in,

81
00:02:38,226 --> 00:02:40,826
on top of the cold syrup already inside

82
00:02:41,358 --> 00:02:43,068
The tank was filled almost to the top,

83
00:02:43,068 --> 00:02:45,468
with about 2.3 million gallons

84
00:02:46,107 --> 00:02:47,777
That much molasses weighed more than twelve

85
00:02:47,777 --> 00:02:49,217
thousand tons

86
00:02:49,451 --> 00:02:50,201
Days earlier,

87
00:02:50,201 --> 00:02:51,441
it had been freezing cold

88
00:02:51,441 --> 00:02:53,795
Then the weather suddenly warmed up

89
00:02:53,945 --> 00:02:55,175
On January 14th,

90
00:02:55,175 --> 00:02:56,935
it was around two degrees Fahrenheit

91
00:02:56,935 --> 00:02:57,615
The next day,

92
00:02:57,615 --> 00:02:59,380
it was over forty

93
00:02:59,530 --> 00:03:01,520
Some historians think the molasses may have

94
00:03:01,520 --> 00:03:02,480
started to ferment,

95
00:03:02,480 --> 00:03:05,080
making gas that pushed on the walls

96
00:03:05,231 --> 00:03:06,701
Inside the thin steel walls,

97
00:03:06,701 --> 00:03:08,657
the pressure kept building

98
00:03:08,807 --> 00:03:10,877
People nearby said the tank often groaned and

99
00:03:10,877 --> 00:03:12,871
rumbled when it was full

100
00:03:13,021 --> 00:03:14,851
And on the morning of January 15th,

101
00:03:14,851 --> 00:03:17,451
nobody imagined what was about to happen

102
00:03:21,250 --> 00:03:22,000
It was lunchtime

103
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:23,280
City workers were eating,

104
00:03:23,280 --> 00:03:25,040
and children were on their way home from

105
00:03:25,040 --> 00:03:26,116
school

106
00:03:26,266 --> 00:03:27,656
At about half past twelve,

107
00:03:27,656 --> 00:03:29,526
the tank split apart

108
00:03:29,676 --> 00:03:31,146
Witnesses heard a sound like thunder,

109
00:03:31,146 --> 00:03:32,778
and the ground shook

110
00:03:32,928 --> 00:03:34,798
Its steel rivets shot out like bullets,

111
00:03:34,798 --> 00:03:37,152
and a wall of molasses burst free

112
00:03:37,302 --> 00:03:39,332
The wave was up to twenty-five feet high,

113
00:03:39,332 --> 00:03:41,932
and moved at about thirty-five miles per hour

114
00:03:42,558 --> 00:03:43,068
In seconds,

115
00:03:43,068 --> 00:03:45,228
the streets were flooded with molasses two to

116
00:03:45,228 --> 00:03:46,751
three feet deep

117
00:03:46,901 --> 00:03:47,811
It crushed buildings,

118
00:03:47,811 --> 00:03:49,931
bent the steel of the elevated railway,

119
00:03:49,931 --> 00:03:52,495
and swept away wagons and horses

120
00:03:52,645 --> 00:03:55,075
A fire station was knocked off its foundation,

121
00:03:55,075 --> 00:03:57,155
with firefighters still inside

122
00:03:57,568 --> 00:03:58,758
A house was torn apart,

123
00:03:58,758 --> 00:04:01,157
and a city building was swept away

124
00:04:01,307 --> 00:04:03,907
There was no time to run

125
00:04:06,817 --> 00:04:08,367
Molasses is thick and heavy

126
00:04:08,367 --> 00:04:09,407
Once it caught people,

127
00:04:09,407 --> 00:04:11,474
it held them like glue

128
00:04:11,624 --> 00:04:13,534
It is almost one and a half times as

129
00:04:13,534 --> 00:04:15,109
heavy as water

130
00:04:15,259 --> 00:04:16,249
The more they struggled,

131
00:04:16,249 --> 00:04:18,116
the harder it was to move

132
00:04:18,266 --> 00:04:20,798
Even strong horses could not pull free

133
00:04:20,948 --> 00:04:22,258
And as the afternoon grew colder,

134
00:04:22,258 --> 00:04:24,584
the molasses grew even thicker

135
00:04:24,734 --> 00:04:26,164
Everything was covered in brown

136
00:04:26,164 --> 00:04:28,044
It was hard to tell where the street ended

137
00:04:28,044 --> 00:04:29,785
and the syrup began

138
00:04:29,935 --> 00:04:31,605
Rescuers waded in up to their waists,

139
00:04:31,605 --> 00:04:33,559
trying to pull people free

140
00:04:33,709 --> 00:04:35,299
Some rescuers got stuck too,

141
00:04:35,299 --> 00:04:37,704
and had to be pulled out themselves

142
00:04:37,854 --> 00:04:39,764
Twenty-one people lost their lives,

143
00:04:39,764 --> 00:04:42,364
and about one hundred and fifty were injured

144
00:04:42,873 --> 00:04:44,223
Among the victims were children,

145
00:04:44,223 --> 00:04:46,623
city workers and a firefighter

146
00:04:49,950 --> 00:04:52,100
Sailors from a ship docked nearby were among

147
00:04:52,100 --> 00:04:53,748
the first to arrive

148
00:04:53,898 --> 00:04:55,728
They were young cadets from a training ship,

149
00:04:55,728 --> 00:04:57,448
and more than a hundred of them ran to

150
00:04:57,448 --> 00:04:58,369
help

151
00:04:58,519 --> 00:04:59,149
Police,

152
00:04:59,149 --> 00:04:59,829
firefighters,

153
00:04:59,829 --> 00:05:02,429
doctors and nurses rushed in to help

154
00:05:02,746 --> 00:05:03,576
Using ropes,

155
00:05:03,576 --> 00:05:05,176
ladders and their bare hands,

156
00:05:05,176 --> 00:05:07,576
rescuers searched through the wreckage

157
00:05:07,750 --> 00:05:10,060
The injured were carried to a hospital nearby,

158
00:05:10,060 --> 00:05:12,232
many of them covered from head to toe in

159
00:05:12,232 --> 00:05:13,174
syrup

160
00:05:13,324 --> 00:05:15,074
Rescue work went on through the night,

161
00:05:15,074 --> 00:05:17,040
by the light of lamps

162
00:05:17,190 --> 00:05:18,340
At the fire station,

163
00:05:18,340 --> 00:05:20,260
firefighters worked for hours to free their

164
00:05:20,260 --> 00:05:21,700
trapped friends

165
00:05:21,881 --> 00:05:24,332
The search for the missing went on for days

166
00:05:24,482 --> 00:05:27,082
Some of the missing were not found for months

167
00:05:27,292 --> 00:05:29,162
It was one of the darkest days the neighborhood

168
00:05:29,162 --> 00:05:30,922
had ever seen

169
00:05:34,250 --> 00:05:36,190
The cleanup took weeks

170
00:05:36,340 --> 00:05:38,370
Hundreds of workers came to help clean the

171
00:05:38,370 --> 00:05:39,490
streets

172
00:05:39,685 --> 00:05:41,795
Workers sprayed salt water from the harbor to

173
00:05:41,795 --> 00:05:43,715
wash the molasses away

174
00:05:43,865 --> 00:05:45,335
Fresh water did not work well

175
00:05:45,335 --> 00:05:47,775
Salt water cut through the sticky syrup much

176
00:05:47,775 --> 00:05:48,777
better

177
00:05:48,927 --> 00:05:50,517
Sand was spread on the streets,

178
00:05:50,517 --> 00:05:53,117
so people could walk without sticking

179
00:05:53,270 --> 00:05:54,740
People tracked it all over Boston,

180
00:05:54,740 --> 00:05:55,500
on their shoes,

181
00:05:55,500 --> 00:05:57,580
into streetcars and homes

182
00:05:57,949 --> 00:05:58,699
Door handles,

183
00:05:58,699 --> 00:06:01,099
benches and telephones across the city became

184
00:06:01,099 --> 00:06:02,165
sticky

185
00:06:02,315 --> 00:06:04,025
The water in the harbor turned brown for

186
00:06:04,025 --> 00:06:05,080
months

187
00:06:05,230 --> 00:06:05,760
In total,

188
00:06:05,760 --> 00:06:07,620
the cleanup took more than eighty-seven

189
00:06:07,620 --> 00:06:09,434
thousand hours of work

190
00:06:09,584 --> 00:06:10,414
And for decades,

191
00:06:10,414 --> 00:06:12,294
people said the neighborhood still smelled of

192
00:06:12,294 --> 00:06:14,694
molasses on hot summer days

193
00:06:18,217 --> 00:06:20,087
Victims and their families sued the company

194
00:06:20,087 --> 00:06:21,609
that owned the tank

195
00:06:21,759 --> 00:06:23,469
More than a hundred lawsuits were filed,

196
00:06:23,469 --> 00:06:25,509
and the courts joined them together into one

197
00:06:25,509 --> 00:06:26,915
huge case

198
00:06:27,065 --> 00:06:29,504
But the company had a surprising answer

199
00:06:29,654 --> 00:06:31,724
It claimed the tank had been blown up by

200
00:06:31,724 --> 00:06:32,124
anarchists,

201
00:06:32,124 --> 00:06:33,383
with a bomb

202
00:06:33,533 --> 00:06:35,563
Anarchists were radicals who wanted to bring

203
00:06:35,563 --> 00:06:37,963
down governments and big companies

204
00:06:38,154 --> 00:06:40,104
There had been real bombings in America at the

205
00:06:40,104 --> 00:06:40,464
time,

206
00:06:40,464 --> 00:06:42,544
so some people believed it

207
00:06:42,694 --> 00:06:43,484
And if it was a bomb,

208
00:06:43,484 --> 00:06:45,772
the company would not have to pay

209
00:06:45,922 --> 00:06:48,478
Its lawyers spent years trying to prove it

210
00:06:48,628 --> 00:06:51,228
It was a clever way to avoid responsibility

211
00:06:51,859 --> 00:06:52,529
But the families,

212
00:06:52,529 --> 00:06:53,329
and their lawyers,

213
00:06:53,329 --> 00:06:55,409
refused to give up

214
00:06:58,683 --> 00:07:00,753
The case became one of the biggest lawsuits in

215
00:07:00,753 --> 00:07:02,193
Massachusetts history

216
00:07:02,817 --> 00:07:04,887
The court chose a special auditor to hear all

217
00:07:04,887 --> 00:07:06,290
the evidence

218
00:07:06,440 --> 00:07:07,190
It lasted years,

219
00:07:07,190 --> 00:07:09,630
with hundreds of witnesses and experts on both

220
00:07:09,630 --> 00:07:10,748
sides

221
00:07:10,898 --> 00:07:12,408
Witnesses described the leaks,

222
00:07:12,408 --> 00:07:13,088
the groaning,

223
00:07:13,088 --> 00:07:14,847
and the brown paint

224
00:07:14,997 --> 00:07:17,387
Engineers studied the broken steel and did the

225
00:07:17,387 --> 00:07:18,400
math

226
00:07:18,550 --> 00:07:20,420
They showed that the steel walls were too thin

227
00:07:20,420 --> 00:07:22,209
to hold that much weight

228
00:07:22,359 --> 00:07:24,549
The company's experts still insisted it was a

229
00:07:24,549 --> 00:07:25,553
bomb

230
00:07:25,703 --> 00:07:27,173
In 1925,

231
00:07:27,173 --> 00:07:28,133
the verdict came:

232
00:07:28,133 --> 00:07:29,053
there was no bomb

233
00:07:29,053 --> 00:07:30,573
The tank was simply too weak,

234
00:07:30,573 --> 00:07:32,578
and had never been tested

235
00:07:32,728 --> 00:07:34,678
The company had to pay the victims about six

236
00:07:34,678 --> 00:07:37,078
hundred and twenty-eight thousand dollars

237
00:07:37,429 --> 00:07:40,029
That is worth many millions of dollars today

238
00:07:43,650 --> 00:07:45,840
The disaster changed how big structures are

239
00:07:45,840 --> 00:07:46,856
built

240
00:07:47,006 --> 00:07:47,796
Before the flood,

241
00:07:47,796 --> 00:07:50,196
a company could build a giant tank with almost

242
00:07:50,196 --> 00:07:52,452
no expert checks at all

243
00:07:52,602 --> 00:07:53,032
Afterward,

244
00:07:53,032 --> 00:07:55,592
Massachusetts and other states began requiring

245
00:07:55,592 --> 00:07:58,192
engineers to check and sign the plans

246
00:07:58,547 --> 00:07:59,937
Calculations had to be shown,

247
00:07:59,937 --> 00:08:02,043
and tanks had to be tested

248
00:08:02,193 --> 00:08:02,903
Over time,

249
00:08:02,903 --> 00:08:05,423
more and more states required engineers to hold

250
00:08:05,423 --> 00:08:06,850
a license

251
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:07,510
Much later,

252
00:08:07,510 --> 00:08:09,750
engineers studied the tank's design again,

253
00:08:09,750 --> 00:08:11,510
with modern tools

254
00:08:11,668 --> 00:08:13,418
They found its steel walls were too thin,

255
00:08:13,418 --> 00:08:15,930
and the metal became brittle in the cold

256
00:08:16,080 --> 00:08:18,150
The cracks probably started near a hole at the

257
00:08:18,150 --> 00:08:19,658
bottom of the tank

258
00:08:19,808 --> 00:08:21,038
Rules that keep tanks,

259
00:08:21,038 --> 00:08:23,198
bridges and buildings safe today grew out of

260
00:08:23,198 --> 00:08:25,161
disasters like this one

261
00:08:25,311 --> 00:08:27,911
A cheap shortcut had cost twenty-one lives

262
00:08:32,117 --> 00:08:33,507
In 2016,

263
00:08:33,507 --> 00:08:36,107
scientists at Harvard studied the flood again

264
00:08:36,820 --> 00:08:37,850
They used old maps,

265
00:08:37,850 --> 00:08:40,250
newspaper reports and weather records from that

266
00:08:40,250 --> 00:08:41,140
day

267
00:08:41,290 --> 00:08:43,680
They even poured corn syrup in a cold room,

268
00:08:43,680 --> 00:08:45,587
to see how it moves

269
00:08:45,737 --> 00:08:47,807
Heavy molasses moves surprisingly fast at

270
00:08:47,807 --> 00:08:48,087
first,

271
00:08:48,087 --> 00:08:50,383
pushed by its own enormous weight

272
00:08:50,533 --> 00:08:52,123
Think of cold honey from the fridge,

273
00:08:52,123 --> 00:08:53,947
compared to warm honey

274
00:08:54,097 --> 00:08:55,727
But as it spread out in the cold January

275
00:08:55,727 --> 00:08:56,007
air,

276
00:08:56,007 --> 00:08:58,302
it cooled and became much thicker

277
00:08:58,452 --> 00:08:59,082
Within minutes,

278
00:08:59,082 --> 00:09:01,082
the flood began to slow down and set,

279
00:09:01,082 --> 00:09:02,923
almost like thick glue

280
00:09:03,073 --> 00:09:04,663
That is why people who were caught could not

281
00:09:04,663 --> 00:09:05,783
escape

282
00:09:05,964 --> 00:09:07,754
And rescuers had to fight through syrup that

283
00:09:07,754 --> 00:09:09,878
was getting stiffer by the minute

284
00:09:10,028 --> 00:09:12,338
Their work showed the old stories of a

285
00:09:12,338 --> 00:09:14,418
thirty-five-mile-per-hour wave were believable

286
00:09:18,317 --> 00:09:18,707
Today,

287
00:09:18,707 --> 00:09:20,787
the spot where the tank stood is a park,

288
00:09:20,787 --> 00:09:22,545
with a baseball field

289
00:09:22,695 --> 00:09:23,444
Kids play ball,

290
00:09:23,444 --> 00:09:25,842
and families walk along the water

291
00:09:25,992 --> 00:09:28,102
There is only a small plaque to remember what

292
00:09:28,102 --> 00:09:29,175
happened

293
00:09:29,325 --> 00:09:31,753
Many people walk past without knowing

294
00:09:31,903 --> 00:09:33,173
In 2019,

295
00:09:33,173 --> 00:09:34,413
a hundred years later,

296
00:09:34,413 --> 00:09:36,293
people gathered there to read out the names of

297
00:09:36,293 --> 00:09:37,558
the victims

298
00:09:37,708 --> 00:09:38,298
Strangely,

299
00:09:38,298 --> 00:09:40,818
the flood happened just one day before America

300
00:09:40,818 --> 00:09:43,418
approved the law that banned alcohol

301
00:09:43,642 --> 00:09:45,512
It is one of the strangest chapters in the

302
00:09:45,512 --> 00:09:47,196
history of Boston

303
00:09:47,346 --> 00:09:49,456
But Bostonians still tell the story of the day

304
00:09:49,456 --> 00:09:51,376
the streets ran brown

305
00:09:51,526 --> 00:09:53,716
It reminds us that safety rules are written for

306
00:09:53,716 --> 00:09:54,894
a reason

307
00:09:55,044 --> 00:09:55,914
A sweet syrup,

308
00:09:55,914 --> 00:09:56,874
a weak tank,

309
00:09:56,874 --> 00:09:59,474
and a company that cut corners

310
00:10:00,250 --> 00:10:01,000
A giant tank

311
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:02,260
A wave of molasses

312
00:10:02,260 --> 00:10:04,020
Twenty-one lives lost

313
00:10:04,384 --> 00:10:06,414
All because a company decided that testing its

314
00:10:06,414 --> 00:10:08,239
tank was not worth the cost

315
00:10:08,389 --> 00:10:10,179
Had you heard of the Great Molasses Flood

316
00:10:10,179 --> 00:10:10,968
before?

317
00:10:11,118 --> 00:10:12,618
Tell me in the comments

318
00:10:12,768 --> 00:10:15,368
Subscribe so you don't miss the next episode
